course: history
lesson: 67
title: Who decides: kings, councils, republics and democracies
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Finn's dad says Magna Carta gave us all our rights. Noah thinks the Americans just stopped having a king one day and everyone got the vote. Ethan says the US has the oldest constitution in the world, and his friend asks «no cap?», meaning «no lie?».
explain: What really happened: Magna Carta in 1215 mainly protected barons and the Church, but it set the idea that even the king must follow the law, and later people used it to argue for wider rights. Revolutions gave the vote at first mostly to men with property, and rights came slowly, step by step.
explain: Think of deciding on a class trip: the teacher can choose alone, a few class reps can choose, or everyone can vote in secret so nobody is bullied. Humans have tried all of these ways to run whole countries.
explain: Many peoples found ways to share decisions: Iceland's Althing met from 930, which Freyja calls «pretty boss», Scouse slang for brilliant; the Haudenosaunee joined their nations in a council of peace; Poland-Lithuania elected its kings.
explain: The hardest thing for a ruler is to let go. George Washington stepped down after eight years as president, and that peaceful handover became a strong example, because for most of history rulers refused to give up power.
fact: Many kings claimed God chose them; in China rulers spoke of the Mandate of Heaven, which a bad ruler could lose, giving people a reason to replace him.
fact: In 1215 English barons forced King John to agree to Magna Carta; it mainly protected barons and the Church, but it set the idea that the king must follow the law.
fact: Iceland's Althing met from 930 and is one of the oldest assemblies; England's Parliament grew from the 1200s, and Poland-Lithuania elected its kings from 1573.
fact: The Haudenosaunee Confederacy united the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca, later the Tuscarora, under a Great Law of Peace; clan mothers chose chiefs and could remove them.
fact: Some say the Haudenosaunee inspired the US Constitution; historians debate this.
fact: In 1776 American colonists declared independence from Britain and later set up a republic; in 1789 the French Revolution overthrew the king's power; at first mostly men with property could vote, and slavery went on in the US.
fact: In 1900 almost no country let all adults vote; today, depending on how researchers count, between about a third and a half of the world's people live in democracies.
fact: In constitutional monarchies like the UK, Japan and Sweden, the king, queen or emperor mostly leads ceremonies, greets visitors and opens buildings, while elected parliaments make the laws; a few kings, as in Saudi Arabia, still have real power.
fact: In 1856 the Australian colonies of Victoria and South Australia began using secret printed ballots, so voters could not be bullied or bribed; many countries copied this «Australian ballot».
fact: The US Constitution of 1787 is the oldest written national constitution still in use, but San Marino has older laws from 1600 that work like one, so it depends how you count.
fact: In 1797 George Washington stepped down as US president after eight years, a peaceful handover of power.
exercise: What did Chinese rulers call their right to rule? | The Mandate of Heaven | The Great Law of Peace | The Magna Carta of China | The Althing of the Emperor
exercise: What could happen to a ruler who governed badly under the Mandate of Heaven? | He could lose it and be replaced | He would live for ever as a punishment | He got even more power from heaven | Nothing ever happened to him
exercise: Who did Magna Carta mainly protect in 1215? | Barons and the Church | Every child in England | Only farmers | People in America
exercise: What lasting idea did Magna Carta set? | Even the king must follow the law | Kings may do anything they please | Every man must own a horse | Parliament must be closed for ever
exercise: When did Iceland's Althing first meet? | In 930 | In 1930 | In 1215 | In 3100 BCE
exercise: Which country elected its kings from 1573? | Poland-Lithuania | Iceland, at the Althing | China under the Ming | Egypt
exercise: Who chose and could remove Haudenosaunee chiefs? | Clan mothers | The King of England | The Pope in Rome | The oldest boys of each clan
exercise: Did the Haudenosaunee inspire the US Constitution? | Some say so; historians debate it | Yes, it is proven beyond any doubt | No, they never once met any Americans | They wrote it all themselves in 1787
exercise: When did American colonists declare independence from Britain? | In 1776 | In 1066, after Hastings | In 1918, after the war | In about 1215
exercise: Who could vote at first in the new republics? | Mostly men with property | Every adult and every child | Only women who owned land | Nobody at all for fifty years
exercise: In 1900, how many countries let all adults vote? | Almost none | Every single one | Exactly half | About 150
exercise: Who makes the laws in the UK today? | The elected Parliament | The King alone, by his own decree | The Church of England alone | The army and its generals
exercise: Which country still has a king with real power? | Saudi Arabia | Sweden, in Scandinavia | Japan, with its emperor | The UK
exercise: Where did the secret printed ballot begin in 1856? | Victoria and South Australia | France, after its revolution | China, under the emperors | Iceland, at the Althing
exercise: Why is the secret ballot important? | Voters cannot be bullied or bribed | It makes counting the votes faster | It lets the king see who chose what | It is only meant for children
exercise: What is the oldest written national constitution still in use? | The US Constitution of 1787 | Magna Carta of 1215, in England | Hammurabi's laws in Babylon | The Althing's book of 930
exercise: What did George Washington do in 1797? | Stepped down peacefully after eight years | Crowned himself King of all America | Started a new war against Britain | Moved to Iceland to join the Althing
test: Ivy says kings who claim God chose them are sus. What did the Mandate of Heaven add? | A bad ruler could lose heaven's support and be replaced | Kings could never be removed, however badly they ruled | Only the emperors themselves were allowed to vote | It made kings live for ever, so nobody could replace them
test: Finn's dad says Magna Carta gave everyone all their rights. Kinda, but not really. Why? | At first it protected barons and the Church; its meaning grew later | At first it gave rights only to children, and adults came later | It was only written in about 2000, so it's too new to count | It actually took all rights away from everyone except the king
test: Freyja: «we had a parliament in 930, pretty boss eh?» Is democracy one invention from one place? | No, many peoples had councils and assemblies | Yes, only Iceland invented it and all others copied | Yes, it came from ancient Rome alone | No, it was only invented in 1990
test: Ava asks if the Haudenosaunee council was like the UN. What is alike? | Several nations agreed to keep peace together | It was run from Paris by the King of France | It was a sports league for the young men | It had 193 members, just like the UN
test: Noah thinks the American Revolution gave everyone the vote at once. What really happened? | Rights came slowly; at first mostly men with property voted | He's right: every man, woman and child could vote from 1776 | Women got the vote first and men had to wait 100 years | Nothing changed at all, and the king stayed in charge
test: Sophie asks how many countries are democracies. Why does her teacher say it depends who you ask? | Experts measure democracy differently | Nobody has ever tried to count them | The number changes every single hour of the day | Democracy is a secret kept by the UN
test: Lily sees the King of the UK on telly and asks if he makes the laws. Answer? | No, he reigns; the elected Parliament makes laws | Yes, he writes every single law by himself at the palace | Only on Sundays, when Parliament is closed | Yes, but only the laws about his horses
test: Kai's grandad: «we invented the secret ballot, mate!» What would voting be like without it? | People voted out loud and could be bullied or bribed | Everyone would vote better, since they'd have to be brave | Nobody could ever vote, mate, because there'd be no paper | Voting would happen only once every hundred years
test: Ethan says the US has the oldest constitution, no cap? What is fair? | Oldest written national one in use, but San Marino's laws are older | No cap, it is older than Hammurabi | Cap, it was written in 2010 | Cap, the US has no constitution
test: Josh asks why Washington would give up power. What would happen if leaders never did? | Changing leaders would often need violence | Nothing would ever change, and that's fine | Leaders would get younger every year | Elections would be easier and quicker
test: In EU4 you pick monarchy or republic from a menu. How did real republics usually begin? | Through long struggles and revolutions | By a king clicking one button on a menu | By a king's birthday wish to his people | By winning a big football match
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the original significance of Magna Carta in 1215? | It chiefly protected barons and the Church, while establishing that the king was subject to law | It conferred universal suffrage upon all inhabitants of England, both men and women | It abolished the English monarchy and established a republic | It was drafted by the Haudenosaunee and carried to England
exam: Identify the assembly that convened from the year 930 and is accounted among the oldest of its kind. | The Althing of Iceland | The Parliament of England | The Senate of the United States | The Diet of Japan
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the secret ballot. | It was adopted in Victoria and South Australia in 1856 to protect voters from intimidation | It was devised in the text of Magna Carta in 1215 and adopted throughout Europe thereafter | It was employed in ancient Egypt for the election of every pharaoh | It permits monarchs to inspect each vote cast by their subjects
exam: Ascertain the significance of George Washington's relinquishment of the presidency in 1797. | It established an example of the peaceful transfer of power | It restored the British monarchy over the former colonies | It abolished the Constitution of the United States | It commenced a civil war between the northern states
